The pad, as the keys the menus and the race already understand.

Every menu screen in ETR registers a `keyb` handler and NULL for the joystick, so rather
than teaching seventeen screens about a controller, one translation covers all of them: a
pad press arrives at exactly the handler the same keypress would, and no screen's own code
changes.

**The race needed three more than the menus did, and had none of them.** The four face
buttons are enough to drive a menu, which is what this was written for. A race is not a
menu: `racing.cpp` charges a jump on SDLK_SPACE, turns a trick on SDLK_t and pauses on
SDLK_p, so with only the face buttons mapped the game was navigable with a pad and not
fully playable with one. The D-pad already steers, paddles and brakes through the hat.

  cross -> Return, circle -> Escape, triangle -> Tab, square -> Backspace
  Options -> p (pause), L1 -> t (trick), R1 -> space (charge a jump)

Indices are `SDL_GameControllerButton` order, which is what this platform's backend
reports (`oops-deps/sdl2/backend/SDL_prosperojoystick.c`): cross at 0, Options at 6, the
shoulders at 9 and 10. The three additions are harmless in the menus, which is what makes
one flat table workable - no menu screen binds space, `t` or `p`.

